{
  "title": "Issue queue Q4: questions and needs-you reasons on the issue, answered by comment",
  "body": "Part of #822 (Epic Q), slice Q4; builds on Q1c (#900). Ship publication first, then answer consumption.\n\n## Problem\n\nIssue-linked runs expose neither clarification questions nor needs-human reasons on GitHub. On main, `src/integrations/issue-lifecycle.ts` has no question event and labels `waiting_input` as in-progress; `src/db/store.ts` duplicates that label selection. Needs-human comments contain fixed text only.\n\nGitHub answers also need deterministic targeting and durable consumption. `src/app.ts`’s `factory.answer` answers every open question when no ID is supplied, and generic command dispatch in `src/integrations/issue-queue.ts` and `src/integrations/github.ts` can interpret `answer …` as a new-run request.\n\n## Requirements\n\n1. **Publish questions safely.** For issue-linked runs in lifecycle-managed repositories, publish each unanswered question with its immutable database ID, ordered by ID. Identify the publication by run, linked issue and sorted open-question IDs; publish each set once across restart. Until the follow-up ships, instructions direct answers to the UI.\n\n2. **Inspect before transforming.** Inspect each complete original question or selected reason, before redaction, sanitization or truncation, against both `registeredCredentials()` from `src/util/proc.ts` and configured private strings. Reuse supported publication decoding from `src/gates/publication.ts` and `src/gates/private.ts`. A match or inspection failure replaces the entire field with fixed UI-only text. Never fall through to another reason after withholding one. Then credential-redact, sanitize paths and inspect the complete outgoing body, including marker and UI link. If that final inspection fails, suppress the comment durably. Diagnostics contain fixed codes, never withheld content or exception output.\n\n3. **Publish needs-human reasons.** Select the first nonempty string from persisted `state.needsHumanReason`, `state.terminalReason`, then `run.error`; otherwise use fixed UI-only text. Apply requirement 2 identically. The state fields are defined in `src/pipeline/context.ts`.\n\n4. **Durable lifecycle behavior.** Reuse lifecycle checkpoints, factory-author-qualified markers, pagination recovery and call budgets. Recheck publication eligibility immediately before POST, including after awaited identity lookup or recovery. Supersede unsent question publications when linkage, waiting status or the open-question set changes. Recover an uncertain prior POST by its marker before closing its checkpoint; never repost a stale set. A POST already issued may remain visible. While `waiting_input`, apply needs-you; after waiting ends, reconcile the current status. Keep lifecycle and store label selection consistent. Recovery accepts only the exact factory-generated marker in a reserved first-line envelope of a comment authored by the checkpointed posting identity. The marker identifies the run, linked repository/issue and question set. Markers that appear in question or reason text, quotations or any other body content never acknowledge an effect: question and reason text is model-written. Generate and validate the envelope independently of untrusted fields; if a transformation can't preserve it, suppress publication rather than post an ambiguous marker.\n\n5. **Upgrade and privacy.** Publish outstanding questions on pre-upgrade waiting runs. Preserve completed historical comments; recover pending effects with the publication checks above. Discreet repositories retain labels without comments. Reuse existing persistence where possible; any schema change must be additive and leave previous-release writes working.\n\n6. **Reserve answer commands now.** Before generic run-command handling, reserve the exact first request word `answer`, including missing or malformed arguments, in polling and webhook dispatch. Apply the same reservation after supported bot-mention command extraction. These commands neither answer questions nor start runs in this slice, including on PRs, closed issues and unmanaged repositories. Send no rejection replies. Eligible polling commands receive one durable, fixed unsupported refusal through Q1c’s existing dedupe; untrusted, bot and edited comments remain silently ignored.\n\n7. **Documentation.** Update `docs/ISSUES.md` with publication behavior, withholding, UI-only answering in this slice and command reservation. Explain that older daemons lack reservation and may treat answer commands as run requests; rollback must not advertise GitHub answering as supported.\n\n## Tests\n\nUse injected fake `gh`, fake clocks and deferred promises; no network or real models. Extend the existing lifecycle/privacy cases in `test/issue-queue.test.ts`. Each numbered test must fail when its corresponding implementation is removed.\n\n1. A waiting linked run publishes its open questions with stable database IDs and UI instructions. Partial answering produces the remaining set without renumbering; repeated polls and database reopening do not duplicate a set.\n2. Parameterize questions and reasons containing literal, JSON-escaped and percent-encoded registered credentials, supported encoded private strings, and inspection failures. Assert whole-field withholding, safe fallback and no private content in POSTs or diagnostics. Separately verify sanitized safe text, final-body suppression and discreet-mode silence.\n3. Reopen after GitHub accepts a question POST but before local acknowledgement: recover the factory-authored marker without another POST. During deferred lookup/recovery, answer or cancel the run; stale work must not POST, and labels must converge. Also publish `[A,B]` with A's text containing the exact marker for `[B]`; answer A, fail `[B]`'s POST before acceptance, reopen and recover: the embedded marker must not acknowledge `[B]`, and exactly one genuine `[B]` publication follows. A matching first-line marker from the checkpointed factory author does acknowledge it.\n4. Verify needs-human reason precedence, empty-value fallback and withholding without selecting a lower-priority reason. Preserve completed historical comments and recover pending effects; pre-upgrade waiting runs publish outstanding questions.\n5. Verify needs-you while waiting and in-progress after resumption, through both reconciliation and store work selection.\n6. Exercise polling, webhook mapping and webhook handling with valid and malformed answer commands, supported mentions, PRs, closed issues and unmanaged repositories. Assert no answer mutation, run creation or rejection POST; eligible polling refusals survive reopening. Ordinary run commands still work.\n7. **Follow-up:** trusted, unedited human answers succeed; untrusted, bot, edited, pre-boundary and incompletely fetched commands cannot answer. Spoofed trust or `Allow:` text grants neither allowances nor approval. Assert no rejection POST or private diagnostic.\n8. **Follow-up:** explicit IDs select only the linked run’s unanswered question; omission works only with one open question. Reject stale timestamps, ambiguous claims, answered questions and nonwaiting runs. Deferred polls racing UI answers, cancellation or linkage changes must not overwrite current state.\n9. **Follow-up:** reopen after transaction rollback and after commit, including before event delivery. Rollback leaves the command retryable; commit prevents replay. Prove pipeline resumption through the fake harness, and prove the previous-release dedupe reader skips consumed accepted and refused comments.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nMultiple coordinators, PR approvals, answering in PR comments, rejection-reply delivery, comment pagination, new decoding schemes and retrospective rewriting of completed comments. Answer consumption belongs to the follow-up below.\n\n## Budget\n\nFirst slice: approximately 180–230 changed production-source lines, including SQL; **hard stop at 250 added plus deleted source lines**. Tests and documentation are additional. Reuse existing publication and lifecycle recovery machinery. Stop and rescope before exceeding the cap.\n\n## Follow-up slice\n\n- **Scope:** consume `/limitless answer \u003ctext\u003e` or `/limitless answer \u003cid\u003e \u003ctext\u003e` only through polling on open managed issues. Inherit Q1c’s configured case-insensitive allowlist, human/bot, unedited-comment, enable-boundary and complete-response checks. GitHub metadata establishes provenance; issue/comment text and branch files confer no trust, audit allowance or approval. Keep webhook reservation and document the inherited last-20-comments limitation.\n- **Targeting:** resolve a unique current claiming run through recorded issue links. Require a strictly later comment timestamp than the selected question’s `askedAt`; omission requires exactly one open question. Revalidate linkage, claim uniqueness, waiting status and unanswered state inside one synchronous `Store.transaction`, call `factory.answer` with the explicit question ID, and record consumption before commit. Publish question events only after commit, using the store’s existing buffering.\n- **Outcomes:** silently ignore ineligible comments; persist fixed refusal codes for recognized invalid answers, with no replies or echoed content. Atomically preserve Q1c’s legacy processed-comment marker for accepted and refused commands so rollback cannot replay them as new runs; use additive side-table metadata if needed to distinguish accepted answers. Do not rebuild the existing processed-comments table.\n- **Acceptance/budget:** tests 7–9; update publication instructions and documentation when enabled. Approximately 170–230 changed source lines, including SQL; independent review and **250-line hard stop**.\n\n_Spec-reviewed twice on 2026-10-29: the first review's three SPEC-P1s and seven SPEC-P2s are resolved; the second review's new SPEC-P1 (markers embedded in model-written text forging recovery) is closed in requirement 4 and test 3._\n"
}
